/li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhang-fei"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/zhang-fei.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Zhang Fei</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Zhang Fei was a general of the late Han and Three Kingdoms period of China, a companion of Liu Bei from the start of his career together with Guan Yu.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhang-fu-ming-general"><span class="enc-name">Zhang Fu (Ming general)</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Zhang Fu was a Ming general who served in the 1406 to 1407 invasion of Vietnam that overthrew the Ho dynasty and created Jiaozhi province.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhang-gui"><span class="enc-name">Zhang Gui</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Zhang Gui was a Chinese official appointed governor of Liang province in the far north-west by the Western Jin in 301.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhang-han"><span class="enc-name">Zhang Han</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Zhang Han was the last great general of the Qin dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhang-jianzhi"><span class="enc-name">Zhang Jianzhi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Zhang Jianzhi (625–706) was a Tang dynasty official who became chancellor under Wu Zetian in his old age.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhang-jinghui"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/zhang-jinghui.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Zhang Jinghui</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Zhang Jinghui was a Chinese general of the Fengtian clique in Manchuria who served the Japanese-backed state of Manchukuo.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhang-jun-former-liang"><span class="enc-name">Zhang Jun (Former Liang)</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Zhang Jun was ruler of Former Liang from 324 to 346, a grandson of its founder Zhang Gui.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhang-mao"><span class="enc-name">Zhang Mao</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Zhang Mao was the ruler of the Former Liang state in the Hexi Corridor of north-western China from 320 to 324.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhang-qian"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/zhang-qian.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Zhang Qian</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Zhang Qian was a Han dynasty envoy sent west by Emperor Wu around 138 BCE to seek an alliance with the Yuezhi against the Xiongnu.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhang-shicheng"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/zhang-shicheng.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Zhang Shicheng</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Zhang Shicheng was a salt-boat worker who rose in revolt against the Yuan dynasty in 1353 and set up the state of Zhou, ruling the rich Yangtze delta from Suzhou.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhang-tianxi"><span class="enc-name">Zhang Tianxi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Zhang Tianxi was the last ruler of Former Liang, who seized power in 363.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhang-xianzhong"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/zhang-xianzhong.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Zhang Xianzhong</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Zhang Xianzhong was a peasant rebel leader of the late Ming dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhang-yi"><span class="enc-name">Zhang Yi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Zhang Yi was a political strategist of the Warring States period who served as chief minister of Qin.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhang-yichao"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/zhang-yichao.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Zhang Yichao</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Zhang Yichao was a local leader of Dunhuang who in 848 drove out the Tibetan garrison as the Tibetan Empire disintegrated, and went on to free other towns of the Hexi…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhang-zhizhong"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/zhang-zhizhong.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Zhang Zhizhong</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Zhang Zhizhong was a Chinese Nationalist general who commanded in the battle of Shanghai in 1937 and later became a leading negotiator with the Communists.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhang-zuolin"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/zhang-zuolin.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Zhang Zuolin</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Zhang Zuolin was a Chinese warlord who rose from bandit leader to master of Manchuria as head of the Fengtian clique.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhangjiakou"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/zhangjiakou.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Zhangjiakou</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Zhangjiakou, formerly known as Kalgan, is a city in north-western Hebei province, China, at a gateway through the Great Wall towards Mongolia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhangsun-sheng"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/zhangsun-sheng.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Zhangsun Sheng</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Zhangsun Sheng was a Chinese general and diplomat of the Northern Zhou and Sui dynasties who specialised in relations with the Turks.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhanguo-ce"><span class="enc-name">Zhanguo ce</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Zhanguo ce, or Strategies of the Warring States, is a Chinese collection of speeches, anecdotes and diplomatic schemes from the fifth to the third century BCE…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhangye"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/zhangye.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Zhangye</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Zhangye is a city in the Hexi Corridor of Gansu province, China.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhanjiang"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/zhanjiang.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Zhanjiang</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Zhanjiang is a port city in south-western Guangdong province, China, on the Leizhou Peninsula.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhao-erfeng"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/zhao-erfeng.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Zhao Erfeng</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Zhao Erfeng was a Qing official and general who imposed direct Chinese rule on Kham, eastern Tibet, from 1905.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhao-mo"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/zhao-mo.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Zhao Mo</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Zhao Mo was the second king of Nanyue, grandson of its founder Zhao Tuo, reigning from 137 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhao-rugu"><span class="enc-name">Zhao Rugu</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Zhao Rugu was a member of the Song imperial clan who served as superintendent of maritime trade at Quanzhou in Fujian.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhao-shanzheng"><span class="enc-name">Zhao Shanzheng</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Zhao Shanzheng was a figurehead ruler of the short-lived Da Tianxing kingdom in Yunnan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhao-tuo"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/zhao-tuo.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Zhao Tuo</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Zhao Tuo was a Qin general sent to conquer the far south who founded the kingdom of Nanyue, with its capital at Panyu (Guangzhou), in 204 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhao-xiangzi"><span class="enc-name">Zhao Xiangzi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Zhao Xiangzi was head of the Zhao lineage, ministers of the State of Jin, in the 5th century BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhao-xie"><span class="enc-name">Zhao Xie</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Zhao Xie was a descendant of the old royal house of Zhao, restored as its king during the revolts against the Qin dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhaoge"><span class="enc-name">Zhaoge</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Zhaoge was a city in northern Henan, near modern Qi County in Hebi, which served as a capital of the last Shang kings.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhaoqing"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/zhaoqing.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Zhaoqing</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Zhaoqing is a city in western Guangdong, China, on the Xi River west of Guangzhou.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhejiang"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/zhejiang.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Zhejiang</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Zhejiang is a coastal province of eastern China, south of the Yangtze delta, with its capital at Hangzhou.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhelyu-zhelev"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/zhelyu-zhelev.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Zhelyu Zhelev</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Zhelyu Zhelev was a Bulgarian philosopher and dissident whose book on fascism was banned under communism.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhenbao-island"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/zhenbao-island.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Zhenbao Island</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Zhenbao Island, known in Russian as Damansky Island, is a small island in the Ussuri River on the border between China and Russia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/zheng-family"><span class="enc-name">Zheng family</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Zheng family was a maritime trading and military clan of south-eastern China, led by Zheng Zhilong and his son Zheng Chenggong, known in the West as Koxinga.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/zheng-he"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/zheng-he.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Zheng He</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Zheng He was a Chinese admiral and court eunuch who led seven great maritime expeditions for the Ming Empire between 1405 and 1433.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/zheng-jing"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/zheng-jing.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Zheng Jing</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Zheng Jing was the son of Koxinga and ruler of the Kingdom of Tungning on Taiwan from 1662 to 1681.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/zheng-keshuang"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/zheng-keshuang.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Zheng Keshuang</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Zheng Keshuang was the last ruler of the Kingdom of Tungning on Taiwan and a grandson of Koxinga.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/zheng-longdan"><span class="enc-name">Zheng Longdan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Zheng Longdan was the last king of Da Changhe, the Zheng family's kingdom in Yunnan that had replaced Nanzhao in 902.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/zheng-maisi"><span class="enc-name">Zheng Maisi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Zheng Maisi was a minister of Nanzhao, descended from a Tang official captured by the kingdom.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/zheng-xiaoxu"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/zheng-xiaoxu.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Zheng Xiaoxu</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Zheng Xiaoxu was a Chinese official, poet and calligrapher who served the Qing dynasty and remained loyal to the deposed emperor Puyi after 1912.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/zheng-zhilong"><span class="enc-name">Zheng Zhilong</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Zheng Zhilong was a Chinese merchant and pirate who built a great maritime trading network from Fujian, Japan and Taiwan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhengdong-province"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/zhengdong-province.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Zhengdong Province</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">Zhengdong Province, the Branch Secretariat for the Eastern Campaign, was an office set up by the Yuan dynasty in Goryeo in 1280 to prepare the invasion of Japan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhengguo-canal"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/zhengguo-canal.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Zhengguo Canal</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Zhengguo Canal was an irrigation canal in the State of Qin, begun in 246 BCE and named after its designer, Zheng Guo, an engineer from the State of Han.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhengtong-emperor"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/zhengtong-emperor.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Zhengtong Emperor</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">The Zhengtong Emperor, Zhu Qizhen, ruled Ming China from 1435 to 1449 and again, as the Tianshun Emperor, from 1457 to 1464.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhengyang-county"><span class="enc-name">Zhengyang County</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Zhengyang County is a county in Zhumadian, in southern Henan province, China, near the Huai River.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhengzhou"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/zhengzhou.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Zhengzhou</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Zhengzhou is the capital of Henan province in central China, a major railway hub near the Yellow River.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhenzhu-khagan"><span class="enc-name">Zhenzhu Khagan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Zhenzhu Khagan, personal name Yinan, was the leader of the Xueyantuo, a Tiele people of Mongolia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhetiru"><span class="enc-name">Zhetiru</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Zhetiru, meaning &quot;seven clans&quot;, are one of the three tribal groupings of the Junior Zhuz, the western branch of the Kazakh people.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhety-zhargy"><span class="enc-name">Zhety Zhargy</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Zhety Zhargy ('Seven Charters') is a code of customary law of the Kazakh Khanate, traditionally attributed to Tauke Khan in the late 17th century and to the leading…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhi-dun"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/zhi-dun.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Zhi Dun</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Zhi Dun was a Buddhist monk and scholar of the Eastern Jin dynasty who moved in the aristocratic circles of Jiankang and the south-east.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhingshak-tseten-dorje"><span class="enc-name">Zhingshak Tseten Dorje</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Zhingshak Tseten Dorje, also called Karma Tseten, was the founder of the Tsangpa dynasty of Tibet.</span></a></li></ul>
